WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.500
SPEAKER: "We the people
of the United States"--

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.042
KEVIN PORTTEUS: We've moved so
far away from the Constitution

00:00:05.042 --> 00:00:07.523
that, in many ways, we
don't even recognize it.

00:00:07.523 --> 00:00:08.016
SPEAKER: "In order to form
a more perfect union"--

00:00:08.016 --> 00:00:09.474
KEVIN PORTTEUS: We
think that we're

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.873
becoming more advanced by
abandoning the Constitution

00:00:12.873 --> 00:00:15.040
and adopting something like
an administrative state.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.373
But what we're in fact doing
is becoming more primitive.

00:00:17.373 --> 00:00:18.150
We are regressing.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.400
SPEAKER: "Establish justice"--

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.740
THOMAS WEST: The
Founders were trying

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.320
to create a regime of liberty,
which would be the greatest

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.600
possible private
scope of freedom

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.670
while at the same
time being based

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.870
upon the basic moral
principles of the natural law

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.199
and natural rights.

00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:34.340
R.J. PESTRITTO: Your rights
of life, and liberty,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.330
and the pursuit of happiness,
these are rights that you have

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:38.900
because you are a human being.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.860
They're not rights that
you have because government

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.750
decides to give them to you.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.080
The idea then for just
government and the only reason

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.410
you would consent to government
is because government

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.716
secures those rights.

00:00:52.716 --> 00:00:56.980
LARRY P. ARNN: The people who
make the laws are people too.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.770
How do we know they won't make
them in their own interest?

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.240
You need a constitution.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.164
And it needs to be in
control of the governed.

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:05.920
JOHN GRANT: Almost
everybody in America,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.260
even people with very
different perspectives,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.460
senses there's something deeply
wrong with our political life.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.640
Recovery of the earlier
constitutional understanding,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.672
I believe it's useful
to help guide us.

00:01:17.672 --> 00:01:19.630
But at the very least,
we have to understand it

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:23.360
in order to know what relation
should we have to that today.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.340
KEVIN PORTTEUS:
The Constitution is

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.030
in the service of a
larger set of principles

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.150
and that when we abandon
those principles,

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.500
not only do we have a different
understanding of rights,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.560
but we also abandon
constitutionalism.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.542
I'm Kevin Portteus.

00:01:37.542 --> 00:01:38.750
THOMAS WEST: I'm Thomas West.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:39.890
R.J. PESTRITTO:
I'm R.J. Pestritto.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.140
JOHN GRANT: I'm John Grant.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.090
KEVIN PORTTEUS: This
is "Constitution 101."

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:43.990
Let's begin.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

